---
import { SITE } from '../consts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="About"
  description="Who writes this site, what the writing is based on, and where the money comes from."
>
  <h1>About</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    One author. Every page here has a point of view — and says what it's based on.
  </p>

  <h2>Who I am</h2>
  <p>
    I'm {SITE.author}. I work in content and data at an internet company — writing SQL, arguing about metric definitions, and automating the repetitive parts of content production. That last one is why I've spent most of the past year inside AI coding and agent tools.
  </p>
  <p>
    The documentation and marketing pages for these tools are excellent. Pointing one at a job with a deadline is a completely different experience. This site is about the second thing.
  </p>
  <p class="meta">
    <!-- TODO: add one line of background plus a verifiable public identity
         (GitHub / X / LinkedIn). Affiliate networks and Google both look for
         evidence that a real person stands behind the content. -->
  </p>

  <h2>What gets written here</h2>
  <p><strong>Yes:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Full write-ups of using an AI tool to finish a specific work task, failures included</li>
    <li>Head-to-head comparisons of several tools on one identical task</li>
    <li>Buying pages that do the work for you: what actually differs, what it costs, what to skip</li>
    <li>The hardware behind the workflow — monitors, docks, machines that run models locally</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>No:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Product pages that add nothing to the spec sheet. If the only source is the manufacturer, it isn't worth your time.</li>
    <li>Roundups with no criteria. A "best of" list is fine — if it says what it's ranking on and what it left out.</li>
    <li>Anything where my day job creates a conflict of interest, including competitors in that industry.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How I test</h2>
  <p>
    Not everything here is a test, and when it isn't, the page says so. Everything
    that <em>is</em> one follows the same rules, so posts can be compared against
    each other:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Same task.</strong> In a head-to-head, every tool gets the identical task — same data, same prompt structure, no swapping the problem halfway through.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Real work only.</strong> I don't build a demo for the post. Demos systematically flatter the tool, and readers can tell.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Failures get written up.</strong> Where it stalled, what it misread, how many rounds it took me to get it right.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Costs stated.</strong> Subscription price, token spend, and the hours it cost me. The hours usually matter more than the subscription.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Everything is dated.</strong> These tools ship a new version every few weeks, so each post opens with a test date and version numbers. If I haven't retested in three months, the post says so.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Where the money comes from</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing, so far. There are no affiliate links on this site today and no
    commission has been earned from it. The
    <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a> sets out the terms
    that apply the moment that changes — including what a commission can never
    buy here, and which recommendations it cannot change.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the record: this site is unaffiliated with my employer, nothing here represents their views, and I don't cover products related to my day job.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If you find something wrong — broken methodology, stale numbers, a conclusion that doesn't hold — please <a href="/contact/">tell me</a>. Corrections go into the original post with a dated note. Nothing gets quietly deleted.
  </p>
</Base>
